{#
  Every alias is generated for the primary domain *and* every alias
  domain, so mail to an address behaves the same whichever of this
  host's domains it was sent to. Previously only the catch-all below
  existed for the alias domains, which silently routed e.g.
  postmaster@<alias domain> to the catch-all mailbox instead of
  forwarding it where postmaster@<primary domain> goes.

  The right-hand side always stays on the primary domain: that is the
  one in Postfix's virtual_mailbox_domains, so it is where the
  mailboxes actually live.

  There is deliberately no `@domain` catch-all. It collected nothing
  but spam, and because Postfix expands virtual aliases recursively it
  also swallowed every address that reached it by way of another
  alias - so real mail ended up at the catch-all target instead of the
  mailbox it was aliased to. Without it, an address with no entry
  falls through to virtual_mailbox_domains and is delivered to the
  matching system account, which is what was wanted all along.

  What this map does NOT do is make the alias domains local. An
  earlier version of this comment claimed it did - "they appear on the
  left of this map, and Postfix derives virtual_alias_domains from
  virtual_alias_maps" - and that is wrong. With virtual_alias_domains
  set to a map, Postfix looks up the *bare domain* as a key; the
  entries below are keyed on full addresses, so `postmap -q lab.test`
  misses and the domain was never in that class at all. It worked for
  the addresses listed here only because alias rewriting runs for
  every recipient regardless of address class.

  The consequence was worse than a refusal, and it is why
  virtual_mailbox_domains in main.cf now names every alias domain
  explicitly. An ordinary account at an alias domain matched no class,
  fell through to the default one, and Postfix tried to deliver it
  *off-host*: `relay=none ... status=bounced (Host or domain name not
  found)`. Verified on the lab by putting the old settings back.
#}
# Virtual aliases for Postfix
{% for d in [domain] + domain_aliases %}
{% for entry in mail.aliases %}
{{ entry.alias }}@{{ d }} {{ entry.forwards | map('regex_replace', '^(.*)$', '\\1@' ~ domain) | join(', ') }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
